  <h1>How to Make Fruit Jerky</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p>Fruit jerky is pureed or thinly sliced fruit dried into chewy strips — essentially a thick, no-added-sugar fruit leather. To make it, blend ripe fruit until smooth, spread it thin on a lined tray, and dry at <strong>135°F for 6–8 hours</strong> until it peels off cleanly. Unlike meat jerky, there's no cure and no food-safety pre-heat — it's just fruit, which makes it the simplest, most family-friendly jerky you can make.</p>
  </div>

  <ContentImage src="/images/fruit-jerky.webp" alt="Homemade fruit jerky strips rolled and stacked on a light board" eager={true} />

  <h2>What Is Fruit Jerky?</h2>
  <p>Fruit jerky is fruit — and nothing else has to go into it. You puree or slice ripe fruit, spread or lay it thin, and dry it slowly until the water is gone and what's left is a chewy, intensely fruity strip. It's the same idea as <strong>fruit leather</strong>, just often thicker and firmer, with a jerky-like chew.</p>
  <p>The important distinction is what fruit jerky <em>isn't</em>. It's not a "fruit snack" or fruit-flavored candy — those are mostly added sugar, juice concentrate, and gelatin. Real fruit jerky is a <strong>whole-food, vegan, no-sugar-added</strong> snack: the sweetness comes entirely from the fruit. That's why it works so well as a lunchbox or trail snack, and why brands like Solely have built a business on exactly this. Looking for the savory plant-based side of the category? Our <a href="/vegan-jerky/">vegan jerky guide</a> covers soy, seitan, and mushroom-based jerkies from the same no-animal-products angle.</p>

  <h2>Fruit Jerky Recipe</h2>
  <p>This is the base <strong>fruit jerky recipe</strong> — it works with almost any fruit. The whole thing is three steps: puree, spread thin, dry low. Because there's no meat, there's no cure to measure and no safe-temperature step to hit, so it's genuinely hard to get wrong. Homemade fruit jerky also costs a fraction of the packaged versions.</p>

  <RecipeCardBlock
