  <ContentImage src="/images/fruit-jerky-puree.webp" alt="Smooth fruit puree spread thin on a lined dehydrator tray before drying" />

  <p>A few things that make the difference between chewy fruit jerky and a sticky mess:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Use ripe fruit.</strong> Under-ripe fruit is tart and watery; ripe fruit is sweeter and purees smoother, so you won't be tempted to add sugar.</li>
    <li><strong>Spread it thin and even.</strong> A 1/8-inch layer dries evenly. Thick or uneven spots stay tacky in the middle while the edges over-dry.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't rush the temperature.</strong> Low heat preserves the color and flavor. Cranking the heat sets the surface and traps moisture underneath.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Fruit Jerky Dehydrator: Time &amp; Temperature</h3>
  <p>A dehydrator is the ideal tool for fruit jerky because it holds a steady low temperature with even airflow. The key number is much lower than meat jerky:</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Method</th><th>Temperature</th><th>Time</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Food dehydrator</td><td>135°F (57°C)</td><td>6–8 hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Oven (lowest setting, door cracked)</td><td>150–170°F</td><td>4–6 hours, check often</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Notice there's <strong>no 160°F or 165°F pre-heat step</strong> like there is for beef or poultry jerky. Fruit carries no salmonella or E. coli risk, so you never have to heat it to a pathogen-killing temperature — you just dry it. That's what makes fruit jerky the best first project for beginners and kids. If you already own a dehydrator for our <a href="/dehydrator-beef-jerky/">dehydrator beef jerky</a>, the same machine handles fruit at the lower setting.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/fruit-jerky-dehydrator.webp" alt="Fruit leather drying on a round dehydrator tray, one strip peeled and rolled" />

  <h2>Organic Fruit Jerky</h2>
  <p>Here's the honest truth about <strong>organic fruit jerky</strong>: when you make it yourself, it's automatically as organic as the fruit you buy. There's nothing to add — no preservatives, no added sugar, no coloring — so a batch made from organic mangoes <em>is</em> organic fruit jerky, full stop. That's the whole appeal of the packaged organic brands, and it's something you fully control at home.</p>
  <p>If organic matters to you, the only decision is the fruit itself. Buy organic fruit, puree it, dry it, and you've matched the ingredient list of any premium organic fruit jerky on the shelf — usually for a lot less money.</p>

  <h2>Organic Mango Fruit Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Mango fruit jerky</strong> is the most popular flavor for good reason: mango is naturally sweet, low in fibrous texture, and purees into a silky, glossy strip. It's the flavor most packaged brands lead with, and the easiest one to nail at home.</p>
  <p>For <strong>organic mango fruit jerky</strong>, use two to three very ripe mangoes (or thawed frozen mango, which is picked ripe and purees beautifully). Blend smooth with a squeeze of lime or lemon, spread thin, and dry at 135°F. Mango needs no added sugar at all when it's ripe. For a little heat — a popular twist — dust the puree with a pinch of chili powder before drying for a mango-chili strip.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/fruit-jerky-mango.webp" alt="Bright orange mango fruit jerky strips on parchment" />

  <h2>Solely Fruit Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Solely</strong> is the brand that put fruit jerky on the map — organic whole fruit, pressed and pureed, then slow-dried into single-ingredient strips with no added sugar, juice concentrate, or preservatives. You'll find <strong>Solely fruit jerky</strong> at Costco, Whole Foods, Walmart, and online, in flavors like mango, mango-chili, pineapple-coconut, and banana. If you searched for it, you were almost certainly looking at one of those organic packs.</p>
  <p>We don't sell Solely — or anything — so this isn't a store. What we can do is answer the two things people actually want to know about it (is it healthy, and how it's made) and then show you that the same snack is something you can make at home from ripe fruit for a fraction of the price. The <a href="#recipe-card">recipe above</a> is the DIY version of exactly what's in the bag.</p>

  <h2>Is Solely Fruit Jerky Healthy?</h2>
  <p>Yes — <strong>Solely fruit jerky is a genuinely healthy snack, with one honest caveat.</strong> It's made from organic fruit and nothing else: no added sugar, no preservatives, no coloring. As a whole-food snack it's far better than candy, chips, or gummy "fruit snacks," and it's vegan and gluten-free by nature.</p>
  <p>The caveat applies to <em>all</em> dried fruit, homemade or packaged. Drying removes the water but keeps the sugar and calories, so they become concentrated per bite — <a href="https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/">USDA FoodData Central</a> figures show dried fruit carries far more sugar per ounce than the fresh fruit it came from. A small strip can hold the natural sugar of a much larger piece of fresh fruit. That doesn't make it unhealthy; it means it's a snack to enjoy in sensible portions rather than by the bagful. The same is exactly true of the homemade version on this page — the honesty cuts both ways.</p>

  <h3>Is Fruit Jerky Good for You?</h3>
  <p>Fruit jerky is good for you as a whole-food snack: it's real fruit, so it brings the fiber, vitamins, and antioxidants of the fruit with it, and it satisfies a sweet craving without the added sugar of candy. Treat it like dried fruit — because that's what it is. Portion-aware, it's a smart choice; eaten mindlessly, the concentrated sugar adds up. If you're comparing snacks on protein and sodium instead, our <a href="/guides/is-beef-jerky-healthy/">guide to whether beef jerky is healthy</a> covers the savory side.</p>

  <h2>Hot Fruit-Flavored Beef Jerky</h2>
  <p>A quick clarification, because these searches overlap: some people looking for "fruit jerky" actually want <strong>fruit-flavored beef jerky</strong> — real beef jerky with a sweet-and-hot fruit glaze, like the mango-habanero and pineapple styles, or the sweet-heat "hot fruit" jerky sold at Asian markets. That's a different product: it's meat, cured and dried at meat temperatures, with fruit in the marinade.</p>
  <p>If that's what you're after, you want a beef recipe, not a fruit one. Our <a href="/recipes/beef-jerky-flavors/">beef jerky flavors guide</a> covers fruit-forward marinades (including a Dr Pepper glaze), and the <a href="/how-to-make-beef-jerky/">how to make beef jerky</a> guide has the full method at the safe 160°F. Fruit-only jerky, covered on this page, is the vegan version — no meat at all.</p>

  <h2>Pineapple Fruit Jerky</h2>
  <p><strong>Pineapple fruit jerky</strong> is tangy, bright, and a little chewier than mango because pineapple is more fibrous. Blend fresh or canned pineapple (drain it well) until smooth, strain out the tough fibers for a cleaner strip, and dry at 135°F. Pineapple is naturally acidic, so it keeps its color well and needs no lemon juice.</p>
  <p>The most popular variation is <strong>pineapple-coconut</strong>: blend a couple of tablespoons of coconut cream into the pineapple puree before drying for a tropical, slightly richer strip. It's the same combination the packaged brands sell, and it's easy to make at home.</p>

  <ContentImage src="/images/fruit-jerky-pineapple.webp" alt="Golden pineapple fruit jerky strips on a light surface" />

  <h2>How Is Solely Fruit Jerky Made?</h2>
  <p>People researching the brand often want to know the process, and it's refreshingly simple: <strong>Solely presses and purees whole organic fruit, then slow-dries it into thin strips</strong> — no added sugar, juice concentrate, or preservatives. That single-ingredient approach is the entire selling point, and it's why the strips taste so distinctly of the fruit itself.</p>
  <p>We don't sell fruit jerky and we won't pretend to know Solely's exact equipment or recipe — but the method is no secret, because it's the same one you'd use in your kitchen: ripe fruit, pureed, dried low. Once you know that, the packaged product stops being mysterious and starts looking very makeable. The <a href="#recipe-card">recipe above</a> is the home version of exactly this process, minus the price tag.</p>
